# Environment variables for @ostomy/mobile.
#
# Expo inlines any variable prefixed EXPO_PUBLIC_ into the JS bundle at build
# time (`process.env.EXPO_PUBLIC_*`) — there is no server-side secret here,
# and nothing in this file should ever hold one: this ships inside the app
# binary and is readable by anyone with the .ipa/.apk. Copy to `.env` locally;
# never commit `.env` (see scripts/check-no-committed-env.sh at the repo root).
#
# Expo is not containerized in the shared development stack
# (docs/deployment-development.md "Mobile clients") — it runs on a developer
# machine or test device and points at the server's LAN address.

# --- API ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# The development server's LAN address, matching apps/api's PORT (default
# 3000) or the Docker stack's published port. NOT "localhost" on a physical
# test device — the device must reach this address over the LAN.
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:3000

# --- Patient OIDC ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Standard OIDC discovery. In development this is the mock-oauth2-server
# container's patient-issuer path (infra/docker-compose.yml), reached at
# DEV_HOST_ADDRESS:8090 — see that file's own comment on why "localhost" only
# works for genuinely single-machine use. In production this is the patient
# Cognito user pool's hosted-UI domain (SRS_v2 §4.6). This app never imports a
# Cognito SDK — it speaks standard OIDC discovery/authorization-code+PKCE only.
EXPO_PUBLIC_OIDC_ISSUER=http://localhost:8090/patient-issuer
# ASSUMPTION (P2.S2a, recorded because it could not be verified against a
# running mock-oauth2-server from this sandbox — no device/browser available
# here): that an issued token's `aud` claim equals the authorization
# request's `client_id` verbatim, with no JSON_CONFIG requestMappings.
#
# VERIFIED AND FALSE (R.S1, 2026-09-20). Run against the pinned image, the
# real Authorization Code + PKCE flow yields `aud: "default"` and puts the
# client_id in `azp` instead. apps/api's guards check `audience` by exact
# match, so every token it minted was refused: `/thresholds`,
# `/sync/delta` and `/value-sets` all returned 401 on a valid signature,
# and this app could not load a threshold, sync, or save an entry. That was
# Gate B's blocker.
#
# The assumption's own instruction was to change this value if the
# behaviour differed. It was NOT changed, and deliberately: the audience
# check is a real control — it is what stops a token minted for a different
# client being replayed against this API — so teaching the API to accept
# "default" would have retired the control and collapsed the patient and
# admin audiences into one string. infra/docker-compose.yml now configures
# mock-oidc with a JSON_CONFIG `requestMappings` that stamps the client_id
# as `aud`, which is how a real IdP behaves.
#
# So the value below is correct AND now actually true on the wire. Leaving
# the original assumption text above, struck through by this note, because
# the thing worth remembering is that it went three sprints unverified.
EXPO_PUBLIC_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=ostomy-patient-app
# Must match apps/api's OIDC_AUDIENCE so a token minted here is accepted
# there (JwtAuthGuard verifies `aud` exactly). See the assumption above and
# src/auth/oidcConfig.ts's top-of-file comment.
EXPO_PUBLIC_OIDC_AUDIENCE=ostomy-patient-app
EXPO_PUBLIC_OIDC_SCOPES=openid profile offline_access
